## Final natural-language hypothesis

> Between 2000 BCE and 1100 BCE, the highest-growth phase of the
> demographic-openness indicator (pollen-based Belau PC1 and its 100-y
> smoothed variants) is centred on the **1400–1301 BCE century** (the bin
> labelled `1400 BCE`), with the immediately adjacent **1300–1201 BCE**
> century forming a comparable or supporting acceleration. Three independent
> centennial growth metrics — the year-on-year growth rate of
> `pollen_inter_100` (mean = +0.00193/yr), the linear slope of `pollen_inter_100`
> (+0.01152/yr), and the centennial change in the 12-indicator capital Z
> composite (+0.679 Z-units) — **all peak in the 1400 BCE bin**. The
> reading is robust to edge-effect sensitivity (full-window and interior
> rankings are identical for the 9 bins considered). The 1400 BCE bin is
> a trough-to-rebound inflection: openness had been declining through 1500
> BCE (the deepest contraction of the window) and rebounded strongly
> through 1300 and 1200 BCE to reach its highest centennial level of the
> window at 1200 BCE.
